I understand. I'll point you directly to the logic blocks in `components/GameCanvas.tsx` that govern the relationship between charging, jumping, and horizontal momentum.

All relevant logic is located inside the **`update`** function.

### 1. The Jump Trigger (Momentum Transfer)
This block (lines **~745-755**) is where the jump starts. This is where you decide how much of the "Charge" speed gets injected into the jump.

```typescript
// Look for this block:
if (jumpRequested && player.isGrounded && player.jumpInputReleased && ...) {
  if (player.isCharging) {
    player.isCharging = false;
    // ADJUST THIS: This sets the horizontal speed for the jump
    player.vel.x = player.facing * CHARGE_SPEED * LONG_JUMP_BOOST; 
  }
  player.jumpPrepTimer = JUMP_PREP_FRAMES;
  player.jumpInputReleased = false;
}
```

### 2. Horizontal Override (The "Momentum Killer")
This block (lines **~758-775**) is the most critical. By default, most 2D shooters set `vel.x` to `PLAYER_SPEED` every frame a key is held. If you are in the air and holding a direction, this line will immediately "reset" your high-speed jump back to a slow walk speed.

```typescript
// Look for the movement handling:
if (!player.isCharging && !player.isUppercutting && ...) {
  if (!player.isDucking) {
    if (moveLeft) { 
       player.facing = -1;
       // ADJUST THIS: If you want to keep momentum, you must prevent 
       // this line from running if the current speed is higher than walk speed.
       player.vel.x = -PLAYER_SPEED; 
    }
    else if (moveRight) { 
       player.facing = 1; 
       // ADJUST THIS: Same override as above.
       player.vel.x = PLAYER_SPEED;
    }
```

### 3. Air Friction
Around lines **~777**, standard friction is applied. If you want a "Long Jump" to feel like you're soaring, you usually want to bypass friction while speed is high.

```typescript
// ADJUST THIS: Standard air friction
if (!player.isGrounded && Math.abs(player.vel.x) <= PLAYER_SPEED) player.vel.x *= FRICTION;
```

### 4. The Landing Snap
Inside the block collision logic (lines **~840-855**), specifically where `overlapX < overlapY` is handled for vertical landings:

```typescript
// Look for the landing logic inside block iteration:
if (player.pos.y + player.height / 2 < block.y + block.h / 2) {
    player.pos.y = block.y - player.height;
    player.isGrounded = true;
    player.vel.y = 0;
    // ADJUST THIS: This "snaps" your speed back to walk speed when you touch dirt.
    if (player.isGrounded && Math.abs(player.vel.x) > PLAYER_SPEED) {
        player.vel.x = Math.sign(player.vel.x) * PLAYER_SPEED;
    }
}
```

By tweaking these specific areas, you can control exactly how much boost you get and whether or not holding a direction key "kills" that boost while Meathead is mid-air.
